    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e1",
      "title": "How French works",
      "body": "French uses the same letters as English, but the letters often make different sounds. The good news: French spelling follows rules, and the rules are very regular. You will learn them one group at a time."
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e2",
      "title": "Three big ideas",
      "body": "• Spelling is not sound. Learn to hear first, then read.\n• The last letter is often silent. petit (small) is said puh-TEE. The t is written, but you do not say it.\n• Every syllable counts. English stresses one syllable (PHO-to-graph). French gives each syllable almost the same length and force, with a small lift only on the last syllable of a group of words."
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e3",
      "title": "Tip",
      "body": "You already read French every day in Canada: on road signs, packages and shop doors. Now learn how those words really sound."
    },
    {
